# Workflow phase discipline

This repository uses explicit phases. Respect the active phase unless the developer explicitly overrides it.

## Recallium per phase

Follow `.cursor/rules/recallium.mdc` and the **## Recallium** section in the active command file:

| Phase | Search before | Save after (if not already in wiki/Git/ledger) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `/stack` | Prior stack or architecture decisions | Approved stack choices |
| `/roadmap` | Prior sequencing or dependency decisions | Approved sequence rationale |
| `/plan-feature` | Prior work on this feature or area | Durable planning context not in ledger |
| `/build` | Unfamiliar conventions for this commit | Durable progress or constraints from the commit |
| `/build-loop` | Same as `/build` for the commit; checkpoint context each loop pass | Durable progress or remediation not in ledger |
| `/fix` | Prior decisions behind delegated findings | Durable remediation context |
| `/checkpoint` | Before presenting checkpoint package | New snapshot memories when constraints, roadmap, or non-obvious learnings changed — not ledger mirrors |
| `/finish-feature` | Feature decisions and constraints | New close-out snapshots when final constraints, roadmap, or feature-wide learnings changed — not ledger mirrors |
| `/spike` | Prior experiments on the question | Spike conclusion not in ledger/wiki |
| `/docs-review` | Prior decisions affecting reconciliation | Rare — prefer wiki; only non-duplicative context |
| `/review` | Context behind findings not in wiki/Git | Do not save — report only |
| `/security-audit` | Prior security decisions or incidents not in wiki/Git | Do not save — report only |

## Stack phase (`/stack`)

Read-only until the developer approves the recommendation:

- Do not edit production code, tests, executable scripts, CI, or `.cursor/` configuration.
- Do not stage, commit, push, or rewrite Git history.
- Allowed: read wiki and repo evidence, external research, present the project-strategy output in chat.
- Write `.wiki/ARCHITECTURE.md` or ADRs only after explicit approval of the proposed stack.

## Roadmap phase (`/roadmap`)

Read-only until the developer approves the sequence:

- Do not edit production code, tests, executable scripts, CI, or `.cursor/` configuration.
- Do not stage, commit, push, or rewrite Git history.
- Allowed: read CONCEPT, planned feature specs, architecture target, present dependency-aware order in chat.
- Write `.wiki/TODO.md` development sequence only after explicit approval. Do not create active feature ledgers.

## Plan-feature phase (`/plan-feature`, or explicit per-feature planning)

Read-only on implementation artifacts:

- Do not edit production code, tests, executable scripts, CI, or `.cursor/` configuration.
- Do not stage, commit, push, or rewrite Git history.
- Allowed: read wiki and repo, create or update active feature ledgers and proportional TODO updates, write scratch notes in `.cursor/work/`.
- Stop after planning. Present the delivery plan (PRs and commits) with exactly one commit marked `Active`.

Cursor's native **Plan mode** (`/plan`) is separate — it does not replace `/plan-feature` for this repository's PR/commit ledgers.

When structural questions arise during planning: read `.wiki/ARCHITECTURE.md`, scan `.cursor/skills/` for matching skills, and search Recallium. If docs and inspection are insufficient and the question needs a **runtime probe**, use optional **`/spike`** — otherwise **ask the developer**. Do not guess and do not implement.

## Build phase (`/build` or implementing the active commit)

- Default: implement **one active commit**, update the ledger (including plan deviations), stop for **`/checkpoint`** and review.
- Opt-in only: full-feature mode still one commit at a time with checkpoint/review between commits.
- Do not stage, commit, push, or run `/checkpoint` unless the developer asks.
- Broad documentation reconciliation belongs to `/finish-feature`, not each `/build`.

## Build-loop phase (`/build-loop` — manual opt-in only)

- **Developer must type `/build-loop`.** Never suggest or run it automatically.
- Same scope as `/build` (one active commit), then automated checkpoint → fix (CRITICAL/HIGH/MEDIUM) → checkpoint, up to **3** fix rounds.
- On success: auto-commit content and ledger advancement (two commits). On failure after 3 rounds: report verdict; do not commit.
- NITPICK findings do not block loop exit.

## Fix phase (`/fix`)

- Address **only** review findings the developer delegated — same project skills and architecture rules as `/build`.
- Do not advance the delivery plan or mark the next commit active.
- Stop for **`/checkpoint`** again. Do not commit inside `/fix`. **Exception:** when `/fix` runs inside **`/build-loop`**, continue to the next automated checkpoint — do not stop for the developer.

## Checkpoint phase (`/checkpoint`)

- Stage exact hunks, run the gate, dispatch the `reviewer` subagent for non-trivial work.
- Present the full **Review verdict** to the developer. Do **not** automatically fix review findings — the developer delegates fixes or approves commit.
- Present evidence and wait for explicit approval before committing locally. **Exception:** **`/build-loop`** auto-commits on loop success without a separate approval step (see `.cursor/commands/build-loop.md` Phase 3).
- After the content commit succeeds, **immediately** update the active feature ledger (real hash, next commit `Active`) and commit that ledger-only advancement — do not leave it unstaged for a separate ask. See `.cursor/commands/checkpoint.md` steps 14–15.
- Do not start new feature work or expand scope beyond the staged outcome.

## Review isolation

Non-trivial staged diffs require a separate `reviewer` subagent (Task) or a new chat with `/review`. The reviewer **reports only** — never fixes findings. Do not self-review implementation you just wrote in the same turn without a reviewer pass.

## Finish-feature phase (`/finish-feature`)

Feature is implementation-complete (all delivery-plan commits done):

1. **Validate first** — full `./scripts/dev test` suite, `./scripts/dev check`, configured smoke/mutate.
2. **Feature-complete review** — reviewer judges the whole feature diff (not `git diff --cached`); present verdict; do not auto-fix.
3. **Docs only after review clears** — `/docs-review` / documentation-steward when Blocking: No or explicitly approved with findings recorded.
4. Do not commit — developer runs `/checkpoint` for documentation reconciliation.

## Security-audit phase (`/security-audit` — optional)

Not part of the core loop. Use for a read-only security pass before deploy, after auth or payment work, or on developer request.

- Do not edit, write, stage, unstage, commit, or push.
- Report only — developer delegates fixes via `/fix` or manual edits.
- `/review` and `reviewer` do not replace this pass.

## Spike phase (`/spike` — optional)

Not part of the core loop. Use when planning or build is blocked until a **runtime experiment** answers one testable question.

- Prefer `.cursor/work/spikes/` or a disposable branch — not production commits on trunk.
- Do not stage, commit, push, or merge spike artifacts by default.
- Record verdict and plan impact; resume with `/plan-feature`, `/roadmap`, or `/build`.
- Research and option comparison belong to wiki, skills, Context7, and `/stack` — not `/spike` unless a small run is still required.

## Trivial work

For trivial changes, the developer may describe the fix directly. Follow the full loop internally without invoking each slash command, but still run the gate before commit.
